WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:06.140
Today, we're going to continue our conversation
about monopoly and corporate power with David

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:12.710
Day in Who's the executive editor at The American
Prospect and also author of the new book Monopolize

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:16.609
Life in the Age of Corporate Power. It's great
to have you on, David.

00:00:16.609 --> 00:00:21.279
Yeah, great to be here. Thanks.
So we recently spoke to Tom Hartman a little

00:00:21.279 --> 00:00:28.510
bit, who's also written about Monopoly, and
he looked very, very closely at some of the

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:34.400
history of monopoly power in the United States.
Maybe we could start with you with a sort

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:40.910
of where we are today. What are the industries
in areas where monopoly power seems to be

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:46.310
sort of the prevailing force industries or
or other areas of American life?

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:53.940
I think it would be easier to tell you what
industries aren't OK, because it's it's a

00:00:53.940 --> 00:01:00.420
very pervasive situation. Basically, look
across the landscape of America and all of

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:06.370
these sectors of the economy and you find
more concentration and more consolidation.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:12.650
And that stands to reason because 40 years
ago, we kind of stopped enforcing antitrust

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:20.350
law and anti monopoly law. And this is the
result. So you have four banks that control

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:26.870
about 60 percent of all deposits for airlines
that control 80 percent of domestic routes.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:33.530
You have concentration in hospital networks
where locally in metropolitan areas, about

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:39.780
90 percent of those are highly concentrated.
You have obviously pharmaceutical monopolies,

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:45.850
the monopolies granted by the government.
You have Facebook and Google and Amazon, all

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:52.030
predominant in their various parts of the
Internet search and social media and e-commerce.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:59.810
So it's hard really to find an area that isn't
tightly concentrated at this stage.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:06.220
Is there a fixed sort of sort of number that
you look at in terms of X number of firms

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:11.340
can can control Y percent of the market, or
does it really depend on the industry we're

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.870
talking about?
I mean, technically speaking, there is there's

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:19.209
this thing called the HERF and Hirschmann
index, which you don't have to know about

00:02:19.209 --> 00:02:27.510
anymore, but it basically means it sets a
number above which a certain level, you know,

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:34.250
a market is highly concentrated. How you define
a market sometimes comes into play with these.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:41.590
And at the at the at the level of enforcement,
sometimes that that line is drawn very tightly

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:48.099
to say, well, if you're talking about local
search in this local area, then maybe that's

00:02:48.099 --> 00:02:53.110
not tightly concentrated. But if you look
sort of with your eyes in reality. And yes,

00:02:53.110 --> 00:03:00.930
obviously a market is concentrated. You know,
there used to be much more precise standards

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:07.690
around this. That there were market share
thresholds where if the Justice Department

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:13.530
saw that a company owned more or controlled
more than 30 percent of a market or 35 or

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:20.870
some number, that they would step in and move
to break up that that that industry or or

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:26.209
deny a merger for those reasons. We don't
have that as much today of this. Hurford del

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:31.769
Hirschmann is very technical. As I say, you
can draw a line in various different ways.

00:03:31.769 --> 00:03:37.821
And so, you know, I prefer to look at this
sort of in on an experimental basis. If you

00:03:37.821 --> 00:03:44.701
go out in the world and you know, you go into
your supermarket aisle and you see the same,

00:03:44.701 --> 00:03:50.030
it looks like a lot of different choices.
But a lot of them are made by the same companies,

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:55.810
you know, try to rent a car, go on a flight,
or do the things that you do throughout your

00:03:55.810 --> 00:04:02.930
day. You find that there's more and more concentration
within those sectors and those service providers.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:10.190
I think a lot of people, if fast. What's the
concern about monopoly power? Overwhelmingly,

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:16.850
people would say, well, a few firms control
a market. They can drive up the price that

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:22.160
I pay for a good or a service. But it goes
well beyond that, doesn't it?

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:27.970
Yes, it does. I mean, it doesn't go beyond
that in terms of how the Justice Department

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:31.500
and the Federal Trade Commission look at things
that they have now, this sort of consumer

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:38.150
welfare standards, and that has really been
devolved into price where if prices are expected

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:43.131
to go down, they approved the merger. But
there's so much more that goes into it. You're

00:04:43.131 --> 00:04:49.040
talking about quality. You know, there's no
incentive to to make a service better. If

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:56.880
there's only one option that a person has
when you're talking about disruption of supply

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:03.830
chains and disruption of service, that magnifies
when there are only a few providers. You look

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:10.900
at airlines that glitch often their computer
systems and they shut down thousands of flights

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:15.610
and a cascading fashion. That wouldn't be
as much of a problem if there were many service

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:22.260
providers, many airline carriers that were
doing flights all around the nation.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:31.050
You look at inequality and particularly regional
inequality. If the large firms on the coasts

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:39.360
are sort of extracting all the wealth from
areas that are said to be left behind, then

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.620
that creates a regional imbalance which has
implications not just for the economy, but

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:53.340
for our politics. If you think about innovation.
If you have large firms that don't want competitors,

00:05:53.340 --> 00:06:00.090
they essentially sit on innovation, find innovators
and either copy their their efforts or or

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:06.130
squash their efforts or just buy them out
and then put them away. In the book, I talk

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:14.970
about a number of these possibilities and
circumstances where price is not the main

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:21.870
driver of what the harm is from Monopoly.
And so I think there's a tremendous amount

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:29.380
of implications to a more concentrated system.
I want to try to draw a little bit of a distinction

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:34.490
maybe between some industries and others and
kind of see where you come out on this in

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:40.039
thinking about airlines, for example. Airlines
are a little different than some other industries

00:06:40.039 --> 00:06:46.500
in that the capital required because you're
talking about outrageously expensive airplanes.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:50.699
Is the capital required is much higher than
in certain other industries, like, for example,

00:06:50.699 --> 00:06:55.669
to open a local bookstore, which might eventually
be driven out of business by Amazon. Can you

00:06:55.669 --> 00:07:01.130
talk a little bit about what type of changes
or solutions you would like to see within

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:08.990
the airline industry? Because it seems very
tough to do anything other than break up airlines

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:14.170
once they get too big because the barriers
to entry to begin. They're just so high.

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:21.190
I mean, what you're talking about there.
And you see this in utilities, for example.

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:27.930
So it doesn't cost. It's not cost effective
to run seven different wires to a particular

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:33.371
residence and have that person pick and choose
between the best service. Right. That just

00:07:33.371 --> 00:07:38.690
would be completely cost prohibitive. So what
do we do with utilities? We heavily regulate

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:45.870
them so that they have a natural monopoly.
But we we put them in a situation where they're

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:52.759
not gouging customers, where they're not providing
substandard service and things like that.

00:07:52.759 --> 00:07:59.789
And so you can sort of extend that out, the
regulated public utility model. And it actually

00:07:59.789 --> 00:08:06.240
historically is how we handle the airlines
in the nineteen. We had a regulating board

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:12.599
called the Civil Aeronautics Board that said,
OK, you can you can have a certain amount

00:08:12.599 --> 00:08:18.921
of profit for a certain level of capacity
on your airline, but you also have to serve

00:08:18.921 --> 00:08:24.780
the entire country. You have to make sure
that if you live in Topeka, Kansas, or if

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:34.789
you live in Portland, Maine, you have access
to airlines and and flights and travel that

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:41.649
allows us to connect the entire country together.
What we did in the 1970s, we deregulated the

00:08:41.649 --> 00:08:47.910
airlines. This led at first to an explosion
of competition, actually. But then at all,

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:51.839
it was it was it was not a virtuous competition.
Everyone was lowering and lowering prices

00:08:51.839 --> 00:08:56.980
and then they completely cost effective. And
then you had this these giant amount of mergers.

00:08:56.980 --> 00:09:02.429
And now what we have with the hub and scope
spoke model is that certain cities have been

00:09:02.429 --> 00:09:08.330
just completely left behind. There just is
no airline service to large sections of the

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:15.839
country and certainly not direct flights.
And as cities like Cincinnati and others have

00:09:15.839 --> 00:09:21.939
had seen there, there are international airports
sort of wither and then seen businesses who

00:09:21.939 --> 00:09:29.319
who rely on business travel, leave those areas
and move to larger parts of the country. So

00:09:29.319 --> 00:09:34.759
this is the sort of with a flick of a wrist
and the change of a where a hub is you can

00:09:34.759 --> 00:09:41.440
turn a city from a world class city into a
second class city. And what the Civil Aeronautics

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:47.399
Board did was it was trying to prevent that.
It was trying to sort of level the playing

00:09:47.399 --> 00:09:52.889
field. And so what I think needs to be done
is that when you have businesses like airlines

00:09:52.889 --> 00:09:59.329
or utilities, you regulate them in such a
way that provides the most service to the

00:09:59.329 --> 00:10:06.110
most people with with the least disruption.
And so airlines is actually a very good model

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:14.689
for this concept of a regulated public utility.
Can you talk a little bit about which industry

00:10:14.689 --> 00:10:20.709
monopolies have been exposed through Corona
virus specifically? Are there are there certain

00:10:20.709 --> 00:10:25.730
specific areas we should be looking where
the the virus and the pandemic have sort of

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:31.040
shined a light on, hey, this industry, that
industry is very much under monopoly control?

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:38.519
Well, I think what's happened is it's accelerated
trends that we've seen for the last 40 years

00:10:38.519 --> 00:10:43.160
for a combination of factors. Number one,
it's simply the case that small businesses

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:48.660
do not have the kind of cash reserve that
large businesses do. We've seen thousands,

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:53.650
tens of thousands of small businesses go under,
thereby giving more market share to the large

00:10:53.650 --> 00:11:01.079
incumbents. The second reason is the Keres
Act, which provided massive amounts of support

00:11:01.079 --> 00:11:07.540
for large businesses, particularly those where
public publicly traded companies by propping

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:14.589
up asset prices, whereas small businesses
got this peepee, which is an eight week fund

00:11:14.589 --> 00:11:21.429
for funding payrolls. Obviously, we're in
the six months month of this pandemic. So

00:11:21.429 --> 00:11:27.959
eight weeks was definitely not enough. And
this imbalance has led to more and more market

00:11:27.959 --> 00:11:34.610
share going towards the top. And then the
third thing is the changes in sort of our

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:42.549
our patterns of life, working from home, doing
deliveries that has benefited the large incumbents

00:11:42.549 --> 00:11:52.389
like Amazon, like certainly the technological
things like Skype, what we're using now, which

00:11:52.389 --> 00:11:57.799
is a division of Microsoft or Google tools
or or things that people are using to make

00:11:57.799 --> 00:12:05.000
the work from home process work. So, you know,
I think I think what I would say is it's accelerated

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:11.870
this trend. And now we're at a position where
five major tech stocks are roughly one quarter

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:17.920
of the. Well, you of the entire S&amp;P 500, which
has never been seen before in history. So

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:25.019
we're we're getting into this position with
fewer and fewer companies controlling more

00:12:25.019 --> 00:12:27.949
and more of the economy.
And it's very dangerous for all the reasons

00:12:27.949 --> 00:12:32.819
that we've outlined in the sort of limited
time we have left. What do you think is responsible

00:12:32.819 --> 00:12:41.339
for getting the US away from breaking up monopolies?
And and actually, it's not even about creating

00:12:41.339 --> 00:12:46.389
law, really just enforcing the laws that that
are there and were enforced more strictly

00:12:46.389 --> 00:12:53.259
for a time. Is it as simple as regular regulatory
capture and the connections between the political

00:12:53.259 --> 00:12:57.759
system and these corporations? Or is it more
more complex than that?

00:12:57.759 --> 00:13:02.800
You can really boil it down to one man. And
that man is named Robert Bork. And he was

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:10.449
the former Reagan nominee for the Supreme
Court. He was a failed nominee for the Supreme

00:13:10.449 --> 00:13:17.110
Court. He had much more impact outside of
the federal bench. He wrote the book, The

00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:25.399
Antitrust Paradox in 1978, which sort of brought
us this consumer welfare prism through which

00:13:25.399 --> 00:13:33.819
to view mergers. His idea was, as long as
a merger makes the merged company more efficient,

00:13:33.819 --> 00:13:40.040
then we should allow the merger. And his corollary
was that any merger makes a company more efficient.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:44.869
So it is perfectly tautological reasoning
at that. That essentially made it impossible

00:13:44.869 --> 00:13:51.339
to stop a merger, and it was internalized
by both the antitrust authorities. The Justice

00:13:51.339 --> 00:13:56.829
Department changed its guidelines in 1982
based on working principles and also the federal

00:13:56.829 --> 00:14:04.470
judiciary, which internalized these concepts
as well. So thanks to Bork and the ideas that

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:09.939
he brought forward with the law and economics
music movement, which was largely out of the

00:14:09.939 --> 00:14:18.029
university, Chicago, we we have changed and
reinterpreted all of these century old antitrust

00:14:18.029 --> 00:14:23.569
laws without really changing a word. What
is what is changing now is we're starting

00:14:23.569 --> 00:14:30.850
to get an awareness of that. And there's sort
of a nascent movement of thinkers and journalists

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:38.420
and academics who are pushing back. Finally
on on this sort of Balkhi and concept of antitrust.

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:44.160
And we're starting to see this bubble up into
the political system. You know, in July, we

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:49.109
had the House antitrust subcommittee, had
the CEOs of all the major tech companies in

00:14:49.109 --> 00:14:55.029
for a hearing which was very aggressive as
to their anti-competitive harms that they

00:14:55.029 --> 00:15:02.209
were placing on businesses and partners that
work with them as well as the public. So I

00:15:02.209 --> 00:15:07.471
think the way that we're going to counteract
this is through a political movement. That's

00:15:07.471 --> 00:15:12.459
really the only way that the antitrust laws
got put in place in the first place. And it's

00:15:12.459 --> 00:15:18.839
the only way that we're going to get a a re
reinterpretation of them, if you will, to

00:15:18.839 --> 00:15:26.100
to restore the concepts that were brought
forward initially with these laws, which was

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:31.279
to preserve, protect and preserve competition.
Well, well said. We've been speaking with

00:15:31.279 --> 00:15:35.489
David De and who is the executive director
at the American Prospect.

00:15:35.489 --> 00:15:39.139
The book is Monopolized Life in the Age of
Corporate Power.

00:15:39.139 --> 00:15:43.471
David, really appreciate your time.
Well, thanks. Thanks for having me. Thanks

00:15:43.471 --> 00:15:44.060
for your interest. The David Pakman Show David
Pakman dadgum.

